// @NAME: Getting Started
// @TITLE: Answering HTTP requests, and the two ways a server can lie about being up.
//
// `server` opens a TCP port and hands you one `Request` per connection. You
// answer each one with a `respond*` method, which sends the reply and closes
// the connection. It also carries a WebSocket server, for one client or many.
//
// It is not the client half — that is `web` (`http_get`, `ws_handler`), which
// this package depends on and which the round trip below uses. It is not a
// framework: there is no router, no middleware and no template engine, because
// a `for` loop over the server and an `if` on `req.path` are already those
// things. And it is not the game wire format — that is `game_protocol`, which
// is built on this.
//
// ## The smallest thing that does something
//
// A server that answers one request, and the client that asks — in one program,
// because that is the shortest thing you can actually run:
use server::*;
use web::*;

fn main() {
  port = 18741;
  parallel {
    serve_one(port);
    ask(port);
  }

  // Every section below is a function, and `main` calls each one. A section
  // `main` does not call still COMPILES — so a renamed API is caught, and the
  // assertions that check the example is RIGHT never run.
  a_bind_that_fails_says_so();
  the_accept_that_does_not_wait();
  binary_bodies_need_respond_bytes();
}

fn serve_one(port: integer) {
  srv = server::listen(port);
  for req in srv {                       // one Request per connection; `next` blocks
    if req.path == "/hello" { req.respond_html("<h1>Hello from loft</h1>"); }
    else { req.respond_404(); }
    break;                               // a real server keeps looping
  }
  srv.close();
}

fn ask(port: integer) {
  // A server that is not up yet answers status 0 rather than failing, so the
  // retry is a plain loop rather than error handling.
  r = http_get("http://127.0.0.1:{port}/hello");
  for _try in 0..50 {
    if r.status != 0 { break; }
    sleep_ms(20);
    r = http_get("http://127.0.0.1:{port}/hello");
  }
  println("status : {r.status}");
  println("body   : {r.body}");
  assert(r.status == 200, "the route answered 200, got {r.status}");
  assert(r.body == "<h1>Hello from loft</h1>", "…with the body it was given");
}

// That prints:
//
//     status : 200
//     body   : <h1>Hello from loft</h1>
//
// `parallel { }` is only here because one program has to be both ends. Your
// server is the `serve_one` half, with no `break` and no second arm.
//
// ## A bind that fails says so — loudly, on purpose
//
// `listen` HALTS when the port is not free. That is the design and not a rough
// edge: a `Server` built on a failed bind is indistinguishable from a healthy
// one at every call site, so the process would report itself up and serve
// nobody — and invisible from outside too, because a readiness probe that
// connects to the port reaches whoever DID win the bind.
//
// When losing the port is an outcome you mean to handle — retrying elsewhere,
// scanning a range — take the recoverable door. `try_listen` never halts, and
// `bound()` is the check that makes its answer usable: every other method on an
// unbound server quietly does nothing, so skipping it buys back exactly the
// silence `listen` exists to prevent.

fn a_bind_that_fails_says_so() {
  srv = server::try_listen(18742);
  assert(srv.bound(), "a free port binds");

  taken = server::try_listen(18742);     // writes one line to stderr, and continues
  assert(!taken.bound(), "a port already held reports bound() == false");
  assert(taken.handle < 0, "…and keeps a negative handle");

  srv.close();
  println("bind   : one holder, and the loser says so");
}

// ## The accept that does not wait
//
// `for req in srv` blocks until a client connects, which is what you want when
// serving is all the process does. `next_nonblocking()` answers `null`
// immediately when nothing is pending, so one loop can interleave HTTP accepts
// with other work — a game tick, a WebSocket pump, a queue drain.

fn the_accept_that_does_not_wait() {
  srv = server::try_listen(18743);
  pending = srv.next_nonblocking();
  assert(pending == null, "nothing connected, so nothing to answer");
  srv.close();
  println("accept : null when idle, so the loop stays yours");
}

// ## The one that bites: a text response DROPS a binary body
//
// `respond` and `respond_typed` take loft `text`, and a `text` built from
// non-UTF-8 bytes is the EMPTY text. So serving a PNG through them answers
// **200 OK with Content-Length: 0** — a success the caller cannot tell from an
// empty file, a broken image in the browser, and nothing reported anywhere.
// Measured: that is how loft's own review viewer served every `doc/images/*.png`
// for a while.
//
// `respond_bytes(status, bytes, content_type)` is the one that carries them.
// The check below is on the value that reaches the responder rather than on a
// served image, because the loss happens at the `text` conversion, before any
// header is written — which is exactly why nothing downstream can see it.

fn binary_bodies_need_respond_bytes() {
  png_magic: vector<u8> = [137, 80, 78, 71, 13, 10, 26, 10];
  as_text = text_from_bytes(png_magic);
  assert(len(as_text) == 0, "a PNG header is not UTF-8, so `text` holds nothing of it");
  assert(len(png_magic) == 8, "…while the bytes themselves are all there");
  println("binary : respond_bytes, never respond — the text form loses it silently");
}

